---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import DurableRun from '@/components/DurableRun.astro'
import { exampleLanguages } from '@/lib/snippets'

const langs = exampleLanguages('workflow')
---

<Doc
  title="Workflows"
  description="A durable workflow in mcpd: the definition the daemon walks, the nine constructs, templates and guards, retries and effects, and the builder that writes it in six languages."
  lede="A workflow is data the daemon holds and bodies the daemon never sees. This page is the data: what a definition can say, and what each construct makes the daemon do."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>The division of labour</h2>
  <p>
    A durable run has two owners. The daemon owns the run between steps: the
    cursor, the journal, the retry budget, the timers and the waits. Your
    process owns everything inside a step: the model you call, the prompt you
    send, the tools you run, the credentials you hold. The daemon is never
    handed any of that, and it never runs a model on your behalf.
  </p>
  <p>
    So a workflow arrives in two parts from one file. The graph goes to the
    daemon as a definition, a document the daemon walks. The step bodies stay in
    your process, in whatever language you wrote them, for a
    <a href="/docs/workers" class="link">worker</a> to serve. Nothing has to be
    deployed to the daemon, and a body can change without the definition
    changing at all.
  </p>

  <DurableRun class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>Writing one</h2>
  <p>
    The builder produces both parts. <code>document()</code> is the definition,
    and each <code>step</code> or <code>agent</code> call keeps its body behind
    for the worker. Every SDK has the same builder with the same nine
    constructs, so a workflow written in one language reads the same in
    another, and the daemon stores the same document whichever wrote it.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" example="workflow" region="workflow" only={langs} title="a support workflow" />

  <p>
    Three things about that example are the shape of every workflow. A step's
    input is a template rendered by the daemon per attempt, so
    <code>draft</code> reads what <code>classify</code> answered without either
    body knowing about the other. A guard decides whether a node runs at all:
    a question is sent as it is, a bug waits for a person. And the last step is
    <code>at_most_once</code> with a retry policy, which is the combination a
    side effect needs: the daemon retries a transient failure, and it never
    runs a body twice without being told.
  </p>

  <h2>The constructs</h2>
  <p>
    Nine, and every SDK has all nine. Two are claimable, which means a worker
    serves them; the rest are the daemon's own.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Construct</th><th>Who runs it</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>task</code></td>
        <td>A worker</td>
        <td>Claimable work. The worker runs whatever code it registered under this id.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>agent</code></td>
        <td>A worker</td>
        <td>The same on the wire, recorded as an agent turn: your own loop, however many model calls, as one durable step. The trace and the interface tell it from a plain task.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sleep</code></td>
        <td>The daemon</td>
        <td>A delay of so many milliseconds. No process is alive during it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sleep_until</code></td>
        <td>The daemon</td>
        <td>A delay until an instant rendered from a template, such as a billing date carried in the input.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>wait_event</code></td>
        <td>The daemon</td>
        <td>A pause until an event with this name and key arrives, or until the deadline passes. The key is rendered per run, so one wait addresses one order and no other.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>parallel</code></td>
        <td>Both</td>
        <td>Named branches, each one claimable step, all on disk before any is offered. The run moves on when the last branch ends.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>foreach</code></td>
        <td>Both</td>
        <td>One claimable step per element of the array at a path, bounded by <code>max_items</code> and, if you say so, by how many run at once. The outputs come back as an array in item order.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>loop</code></td>
        <td>Both</td>
        <td>A bounded loop over one claimable step. <code>until</code> is evaluated after each iteration and may end it early.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>subworkflow</code></td>
        <td>The daemon</td>
        <td>A child run of another definition, with its own journal. Its output becomes this step's output, and cancelling the parent cancels it.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A branch, a foreach body and a loop body are one claimable step each. A
    body that needs several steps is a subworkflow, which keeps every journal
    flat: a run is a list, never a tree of lists.
  </p>

  <h2>Templates and guards</h2>
  <p>
    A template is JSON with <code>${'${path}'}</code> references in its strings.
    The daemon renders it when an attempt is created, from what the run holds
    at that moment: <code>input</code>, the run's input;
    <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;</code>, what an earlier step answered, with
    <code>.output</code>, <code>.status</code> and <code>.error</code>;
    <code>run</code>, the run's own id and correlation; and inside a foreach or
    a loop, <code>item</code>, <code>index</code> and <code>iter.index</code>.
    A reference that is the whole string keeps its type, so
    <code>"${'${steps.classify.output}'}"</code> hands the next step an object,
    not a string of one.
  </p>
  <p>
    A guard is a predicate on the same paths: <code>eq</code>, <code>ne</code>,
    <code>lt</code>, <code>lte</code>, <code>gt</code>, <code>gte</code>,
    <code>in</code>, <code>exists</code> and <code>matches</code>, combined with
    <code>all</code>, <code>any</code> and <code>not</code>. A guard that is
    false writes the node as <code>skipped</code> and the run moves on. A path
    that is absent makes every comparison false rather than raising, because a
    run that dies on a missing field is a run nobody can finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    The daemon validates the document when it is stored. A guard that names a
    path nothing can produce, a template that reads a step declared later, a
    foreach over something that is not a path, an id that does not match
    <code>^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$</code>: each is refused with a 400 naming the
    field. Unknown fields are refused too, so a guard spelled wrong is an error
    and not a run that skips its guard.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every duration has a ceiling as well as a floor. <code>run_timeout_ms</code>,
    <code>step_timeout_ms</code>, a retry's <code>initial_ms</code> and
    <code>max_ms</code>, a sleep's <code>ms</code> and a wait's
    <code>timeout_ms</code> are each at most <code>315360000000</code>, which
    is 3650 days, and a longer one is refused with a 400 naming the field, the
    ceiling and the value you sent. A deadline further out than that would not
    fit the clock the daemon stores it on.
  </p>

  <h2>Retries, effects and failure</h2>
  <p>
    A body that fails tells the daemon how, with a class. <code>retryable</code>
    is worth another attempt, and the daemon makes one while the node's
    <code>retry</code> budget lasts, at the node's backoff, waiting at least
    the <code>retry_after_ms</code> the body asked for, which is held to the
    same 3650-day ceiling as every other duration. <code>fatal</code> and
    <code>invalid_input</code> are not retried. <code>unservable</code> means
    this worker does not hold the definition or the step, and the attempt goes
    back to the queue for another worker without spending the budget. A body
    that throws anything else is <code>fatal</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>max_attempts</code> counts every attempt including the first, so the
    default of one means no retry. After the budget, <code>on_error</code>
    decides: <code>fail_run</code>, the default, ends the run with the step's
    message as its error; <code>continue</code> marks the step failed and
    moves on, so a later guard can read <code>steps.&lt;id&gt;.status</code>
    and route around it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>effect</code> says what a duplicated body would cost. Under
    <code>at_least_once</code>, the default, a lease that is lost requeues the
    attempt and the body may run again. Under <code>at_most_once</code> the
    worker must post <code>started</code> before the body, and a started
    attempt whose lease is lost fails rather than runs again. Use it for the
    step that sends the email or charges the card, and give that body the
    lease's <code>idempotency_key</code> to hand to the third party: it is
    stable across retries of the same step, so a retry after a lost reply is
    recognised on the far side too.
  </p>

  <h2>Versions</h2>
  <p>
    A definition is immutable once stored. Post the same body again and you
    get the same version back; post a different body under a version that
    exists and the daemon refuses with a 409, because a run that pinned that
    version would otherwise change under it. Leave the version out and the
    daemon assigns the next number, which is what a worker does at start-up.
    A run pins the version it started with and never re-resolves it, so a
    deploy that stores version 3 finishes the runs on version 2 as they were
    written.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>
    A definition may declare <code>max_steps</code>, <code>run_timeout_ms</code>
    and <code>max_cost_usd</code>. The daemon counts every attempt against the
    first, the run's age against the second, and the priced usage the steps
    report against the third, and ends the run <code>timed_out</code> or
    <code>failed</code> at the ceiling with the ceiling named as the error. A
    loop that will not converge and an agent step that will not stop spending
    are the same problem to the daemon, and the same flag stops both.
  </p>

  <h2>What the daemon keeps</h2>
  <p>
    A run's input, its state, its output and every step's input and output are
    kept in the run store exactly as they were sent. They are not redacted:
    they are the workflow's own working state, a later step reads them back,
    and a value the redactor had rewritten would change what the run does. The
    spans a run writes into the trace store are redacted like any other span.
    Retention and erasure reach the run store as well, for runs that have
    ended. <a href="/docs/runs#storage" class="link">Runs</a> says exactly
    what each one deletes.
  </p>

